      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "HISTORY AND CLINICAL FINDINGS",
          "content": "A 44-year-old manager presented himself for the assessment of nocturnal apnoea. He reported increasing lack of drive and nightly angina pectoris."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INVESTIGATIONS",
          "content": "Polysomnography indicated obstructive apnoea and hypopnoea with a respiratory disturbance index of 29.1, while the ECG showed T wave inversion in all leads. There was severe hypothyroidism with atrophic thyroid tissue."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DIAGNOSIS, TREATMENT AND COURSE",
          "content": "The patient had an obstructive sleep apnoea syndrome (OSAS), hypothyroidism with myxoedema, hypopnoea and myocardial ischaemia. He was treated with negative peak airway pressure (nCPAP) ventilation, administration of L-thyroxine and initiation of anti-anginal medication, which relieved his symptoms. The severe hypothyroidism was thought to be the most important cause of his respiratory disorder and angina."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "This case illustrates the connection between hypothyroidism and OSAS. Hypothyroidism must be excluded in patients with OSAS, regardless of the patient's age."
        }
      ]},
